<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The video surface for one zone — the FAST path, used whenever nobody is watching this screen
    remotely, which is essentially always.

    surface_type="surface_view" puts the decoded video on its own hardware layer, composited by
    SurfaceFlinger without ever passing through the app's GPU pipeline. That is what a set-top box
    is built to do: the decoder writes straight to the display and the app spends nothing per frame.

    The TextureView alternative (zone_player_view_capture.xml) costs an extra full-frame GPU copy at
    the video's frame rate. On a 2GB Android TV box driving 1080p that is not "a few percent of
    GPU", which an earlier comment here claimed — it is the difference between smooth playback and
    visible judder, and it was being paid on every screen in the fleet, permanently, to make a
    screenshot feature work that almost nobody was using at any given moment.

    So the surface type now follows the Live Data View toggle: this layout by default, and the
    TextureView one only while an operator actually has the panel open. See ZoneRenderer.VideoLayer.

    surface_type can only be set here — PlayerView reads it at inflation and it cannot be changed in
    code afterwards — which is why there are two files rather than one builder call.
-->
<androidx.media3.ui.PlayerView xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:background="@android:color/transparent"
    app:surface_type="surface_view"
    app:use_controller="false"
    app:hide_on_touch="false"
    app:resize_mode="fit" />
